    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d282">

                <pb facs="0435" n="417" sameAs="frus:frus1946v05/pg417"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">851.51/3–346: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Caffery</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                        <lb/>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">us urgent</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Paris</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1946-03-03T12:00:00+01:00">March 3,
                        1946—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received March 3—10:55 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1031. Léon Blum (whose condition is somewhat improved although he is still
                    running a fever) sent me a message last evening that he regretted exceedingly
                    that the departure of the French delegation had <pb facs="0436" n="418"
                        xml:id="pg418"/> been delayed (my 1029, March 2<anchor n="34"
                        xml:id="d282-f1"/>). He said that in as much as no planes will be able to
                    take off from the Paris airfields because of the heavy snow until at the
                    earliest Monday evening, the delegation would arrive in Washington too late to
                    have conferences with Secretary Vinson and Assistant Secretary Clayton before
                    their departure for Savannah. Under these circumstances the decision had been
                    made to postpone the delegation’s departure until the conclusion of the Savannah
                    Conference.</p>
                <p>Blum asked me to convey to Secretary Byrnes, Secretary Vinson, and Assistant
                    Secretary Clayton his deep regret that his illness and bad weather had
                    necessitated a change in plans. He asked particularly that his apologies be
                    conveyed for any inconvenience that may have been caused to the Secretary,
                    Secretary Vinson, and Assistant Secretary Clayton.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Caffery</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d282-f1" n="34" xml:id="d282-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed. Later the Department was informed in telegram 1177, March 11, from
                    Paris, that Mr. Blum would leave Paris for Washington on March 14
                    (851.51/3–1146).</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
